# Consider the following Algorithms 
# 1) Time Complexity
# linear_search
# for item in my_list:
#    if item == target:
#        return True


# Worst case: checks every element → O(n)

# Average case: still proportional to n → O(n)

# Best case: first element matches → O(1)

# Space: O(1)

# binary_search

# Each step eliminates half of the remaining elements.

# Worst case: O(log n)

# Average case: O(log n)

# Best case: middle is the target → O(1)

# Space: O(1) (iterative version)

# 2) When should you use each one?
# Use linear search when:

# the list is small
# the list is not sorted
# you will perform very few searches
# data changes frequently

# Use binary search when:

# the list is sorted (mandatory)
# the dataset is large
# you will run many searches
# you can afford sorting once and searching many times

# 3) What happens if the list is NOT sorted?

# Binary search depends on order.

# If the list is not sorted:
# it can return False even if the element exists
# results are unreliable

# In that case, you must:

# either use linear search
# or sort first, then apply binary search.

# Summary 
# linear_search  → O(n)
# binary_search  → O(log n)

# Binary search is more efficient for large, sorted datasets.
# If the list is not sorted, binary search cannot be used safely.
